#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158290} :activity/id #uuid "55c2127a-d43f-53c5-907e-c8d12316517c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048747 :topic/id #uuid "bac6604a-5262-5144-8ceb-19f17d508317" :topic/math-academy-id 2987} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158290 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158562 :tutorial/content "Suppose $X$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S.$\n\nThe values $\\textrm{E}[X]$ and $\\textrm{E}[X^2]$ are called the **first moment** and **second moment** of $X,$ respectively. \n\nRecall that the first moment $\\textrm{E}[X]$ is also called the *expected value* of $X$ and is computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\textrm{E}[X] = \\int_S x \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nTo find second moment $\\textrm{E}[X^2],$ the expected value of the the random variable $X^2,$ we simply substitute $x^2$ for $x$ in the formula above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\textrm{E}[X^2] = \\int_S x^2 \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nLet's compute the second moment $\\textrm{E}[X^2]$ of the continuous random variable $X$ with the probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}, \\qquad 0 \\leq x \\leq 1.\n$$\n\nWe compute the expected value of $X^2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{5/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{2}{7}{x}^{7/2}){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{2}{7}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2847fd82-e842-5ae8-889a-66f29c0442ec" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6591 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3dc1ae12-0989-5596-8114-7d74427919d2" :step/math-academy-id 39753 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158292}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158291 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157393 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156994 :question/id #uuid "65957407-b302-51c5-93d9-0f71f3517362" :question/math-academy-id "e-10527"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9c799d6-e3fe-5b78-986d-f2068b2db058" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050775}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157606} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157991} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427364} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427369} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434427} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434428} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479144} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508043} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508093}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Mean of a Transformed Random Variable"} :step/id #uuid "897313ee-485e-51e5-8fec-b665d482b874" :step/math-academy-id 39754} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158292 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157394 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156995 :question/id #uuid "292610ec-9c27-590d-b207-c76adc959d55" :question/math-academy-id "e-10528"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bada6a7f-c02e-51cd-8ea2-0594a94648a1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048516} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049801}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157602} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157603} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427360} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427361} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427368} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434425}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Second Moment of a Random Variable"} :step/id #uuid "08325c1e-fe0c-5a7b-b6e5-8f27c2951461" :step/math-academy-id 39755 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158294}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158293 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157395 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156996 :question/id #uuid "4c5666a4-f477-5b1a-8d17-1e2aa594863f" :question/math-academy-id "e-10529"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88bd32d5-4a84-58b3-a3e9-73bf32cb5e4f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157604} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157605} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427362} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427363} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427365} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427366} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434426} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479070} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479115}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Higher Moments of a Random Variable"} :step/id #uuid "bce87273-1a01-58a6-825f-736ef62ef788" :step/math-academy-id 39756 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158291}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158294 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158563 :tutorial/content "It's also possible to define higher moments. For example, $\\textrm{E}[X^3]$ is the **third moment** of the random variable $X.$\n\nNotice the pattern:\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\textrm{E}[X] = \\int_S x \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x$ is the *first* moment\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\textrm{E}[X^2] = \\int_S x^2 \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x$ is the *second* moment\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\textrm{E}[X^3] = \\int_S x^3 \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x$ is the *third* moment\n\nand so on. In general, the **$n$th moment** is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X^n] = \\int_S x^n \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nFinally, note that \n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X], \\quad \\textrm{E}[X^2], \\quad \\textrm{E}[X^3], \\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nare sometimes referred to as the first, second, and third **raw moments**. This is because it's possible to define other types of moments.\n\nIn general, $\\textrm{E}[X^n]$ can be called either the $n$th *moment* or $n$th *raw moment*." :tutorial/id #uuid "e3527d5e-b0d3-5d76-aa5f-5bc7ffcfd66b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6592 :tutorial/title "Higher Moments"} :step/id #uuid "9fb3611b-a9ba-58f4-9159-00a7c9b29393" :step/math-academy-id 39757 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158293}}] :activity/title "Moments of Continuous Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186157533}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479151} :activity/id #uuid "f961c2fd-b375-5e2b-8cfd-ec028dc7aa0d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048747 :topic/id #uuid "bac6604a-5262-5144-8ceb-19f17d508317" :topic/math-academy-id 2987} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479149 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479115 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85e8f87d-a86b-5e5a-9b4c-520af1d67778" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478767} {:answer/id #uuid "8a5de172-0879-5846-8775-50301538400b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478775} {:answer/id #uuid "8c404e33-978f-5492-8afd-92388f08864c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478781} {:answer/id #uuid "92a6058f-3718-55b4-83a2-afc873b42494" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478797} {:answer/id #uuid "a7862d49-f18d-5362-98d3-095ce5b8964a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{13}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478832}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478832} :answer-field/id #uuid "63b35dd5-9730-5404-b881-f84b63c0e854" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478998}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ac78b028-e0aa-5c92-b54a-b1ba1527a82f" :question/math-academy-id "q-152915" :question/problem "A continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{4}(1-x),\\,-2\\le x\\le 0.\n$$\n\nCalculate $\\text{E}[{X}^{3}].$" :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the third raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{3}] & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}{x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}(1-x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}({x}^{3}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}-\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}){|}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(0-[4+\\frac{32}{5}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(-\\frac{52}{5}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{13}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "121da9a3-a27d-57e3-b7b6-52d9b4081634" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479157}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479151 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427364 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33cb4776-7d73-5e89-be66-3abb80c22cd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{e}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427291} {:answer/id #uuid "5d5bf664-a18f-566d-a4f7-3bd738b55f2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427301} {:answer/id #uuid "74d74ac0-e401-549d-95e1-5b551db31568" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427309} {:answer/id #uuid "aa2240d8-61b4-5dec-86e4-1ecedc83ab1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427322} {:answer/id #uuid "ce1156f5-fddd-5d6b-adf1-9732ee27312f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427333}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427291} :answer-field/id #uuid "62e8ebaf-8a75-5cb6-8621-5bcf5f7ac9e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427348}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "63bcffd8-dbf0-5c48-8ca6-56531c05d1c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-131141" :question/problem "The continuous random variable $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{2x},\\,{e}^{-2}<x<1\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2}},$ compute $\\text{E}[4{X}^{2}-2X].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that $\\text{E}[aX+bY]=a\\text{E}[X]+b\\text{E}[Y]$ for any random variables $X$ and $Y$ and constants $a$ and $b.$*" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify using the hint and the properties of expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[4{X}^{2}-2X]=4\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-2\\text{E}[X]\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe already know $\\text{E}[X].$ So, we compute the second raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{e}^{-2}}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{e}^{-2}}^{1}\\frac{x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}\\,|}_{{e}^{-2}}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{4{e}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2}}$ and $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]=\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{4{e}^{4}}$ into the simplified expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[4{X}^{2}-2X] & =4\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-2\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & =4(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{4{e}^{4}})-2(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2}}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{4}}-1+\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{e}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1ec8cc12-b2a4-5daf-96fd-1e378052ed2d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479152}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479152 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479144 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cfd005b-75a8-5b0f-ba56-4a0ed93ab33a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478604} {:answer/id #uuid "22afcee8-e19d-562c-b205-332bc6a88919" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478613} {:answer/id #uuid "7a90c263-2858-513a-872f-0ae63784d35a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478741} {:answer/id #uuid "edf857b5-69b2-5c68-8336-4b0f3c36ec60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478931} {:answer/id #uuid "ff85e4b7-ab02-503c-8793-d2b775c75049" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478958}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478958} :answer-field/id #uuid "623ea09b-af47-50f2-9745-0713c7359869" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478997}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f6cddda8-c777-5283-b03a-3671533a782a" :question/math-academy-id "q-131153" :question/problem "Given that $X$ is a continuous random variable with the following probability density function, compute $\\text{E}[10-4{X}^{2}].$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}},\\,1<x<2\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify using the properties of expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[10-4{X}^{2}]=10-4\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the second raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2x\\,{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]=2$ into the simplified expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[10-4{X}^{2}] & =10-4\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] \\\\  & =10-4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "46c499f6-0d50-5bd9-9f52-26ed8cb8ebb6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479153}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479153 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157606 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a69b927d-fd51-516f-aae0-cf0da09a7720" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153881} {:answer/id #uuid "e3ba8dfc-c937-598b-a9a5-dc59f7c01e72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153882} {:answer/id #uuid "eb402453-8e9e-5d3c-a06e-96fbfc84d9da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153883} {:answer/id #uuid "f418ec1e-ed9d-5bce-a47f-762f1c808098" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153884} {:answer/id #uuid "63a0df93-12b0-5a94-8153-cab57c777d0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153885}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153883} :answer-field/id #uuid "07e9af8f-c53a-563f-b1d5-c1fb676708fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153880}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d40b16f4-c022-5679-aaee-aa1be46926d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-149708" :question/problem "The continuous random variable $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{3{x}^{2}+4}{5},\\,0<x<1\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{11}{20},$ compute $\\text{E}[2X-{X}^{3}+1].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that $\\text{E}[aX+bY]=a\\text{E}[X]+b\\text{E}[Y]$ for any random variables $X$ and $Y$ and constants $a$ and $b.$*" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify using the hint and the properties of expected value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[2X-{X}^{3}+1] & =2\\text{E}[X]-\\text{E}[{X}^{3}]+\\text{E}[1] \\\\  & =2\\text{E}[X]-\\text{E}[{X}^{3}]+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe already know $\\text{E}[X].$ So, we compute the third raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{3}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}\\cdot (\\frac{3{x}^{2}+4}{5})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{3}{5}{x}^{5}+\\frac{4}{5}{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{10}{x}^{6}+\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{4})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}+\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{11}{20}$ and $\\text{E}[{X}^{3}]=\\frac{3}{10}$ into the simplified expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[2X-{X}^{3}+1] & =2\\text{E}[X]-\\text{E}[{X}^{3}]+1 \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{11}{20}-\\frac{3}{10}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{10}-\\frac{3}{10}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "473a0974-6e51-5843-94d7-0cf17d7cd776" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479149}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479157 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479070 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1370ffbd-2eab-5260-a0b4-0bf5d3cdb6db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478582} {:answer/id #uuid "7f3f6cb5-53fa-5af5-81d3-830801107641" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478750} {:answer/id #uuid "82d2a8f9-d415-5d71-a7d9-fe699cab072f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478758} {:answer/id #uuid "bedb09af-8e03-5cb2-a39f-8ac2f91ce3a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478867} {:answer/id #uuid "e89460a9-557b-5055-9a71-6a052e322419" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478921}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478867} :answer-field/id #uuid "aded51f8-58f0-5888-884c-244971621142" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479026}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2317ff10-80a3-5747-858a-863cca47fbc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-152922" :question/problem "A continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}, & 0\\le x<1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate $\\text{E}[{X}^{3}].$" :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the third raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{3}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}\\,{x}^{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}+{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{x}){|}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}(1-0)+{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(1-\\frac{1}{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}+(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b0bf133b-cce4-5d5c-905a-ed7bcb9e9bdc"}] :activity/title "Moments of Continuous Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478561}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508148} :activity/id #uuid "3d4cf227-5451-5675-a3d0-310e1e202b0c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048747 :topic/id #uuid "bac6604a-5262-5144-8ceb-19f17d508317" :topic/math-academy-id 2987} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508118 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434426 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d8146a1-89ab-5805-b0d1-3cb41fad7d9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{8}-1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434401} {:answer/id #uuid "77b5e97e-bc6c-5d70-b0ab-61c62f43eefe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{4}-1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434406} {:answer/id #uuid "b2c4a89b-c4d2-5db7-98e5-df1b9a18f200" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8e-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434414} {:answer/id #uuid "bcd85839-7391-5b9a-80bf-f734af24df0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{e}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434415} {:answer/id #uuid "c0834f80-0b5a-5af1-a67a-b29a8e35f4d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434416}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434401} :answer-field/id #uuid "29094946-ea57-508c-a265-54b76fd27fdf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434422}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1a18a122-3ad0-51db-8855-5d861bdec3e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-152917" :question/problem "A continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{2x},\\,1\\le x\\le {e}^{2}.\n$$\n\nCalculate $\\text{E}[{X}^{4}].$" :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the fourth raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{4}] & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}{x}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\,{x}^{4}\\,{|}_{1}^{{e}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}({e}^{8}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{8}-1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0aef0e95-7b36-56cc-a917-a6909d024646"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508137 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479115 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85e8f87d-a86b-5e5a-9b4c-520af1d67778" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478767} {:answer/id #uuid "8a5de172-0879-5846-8775-50301538400b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478775} {:answer/id #uuid "8c404e33-978f-5492-8afd-92388f08864c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478781} {:answer/id #uuid "92a6058f-3718-55b4-83a2-afc873b42494" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478797} {:answer/id #uuid "a7862d49-f18d-5362-98d3-095ce5b8964a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{13}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478832}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478832} :answer-field/id #uuid "63b35dd5-9730-5404-b881-f84b63c0e854" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478998}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ac78b028-e0aa-5c92-b54a-b1ba1527a82f" :question/math-academy-id "q-152915" :question/problem "A continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{4}(1-x),\\,-2\\le x\\le 0.\n$$\n\nCalculate $\\text{E}[{X}^{3}].$" :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the third raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{3}] & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}{x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}(1-x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}({x}^{3}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}-\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}){|}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(0-[4+\\frac{32}{5}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(-\\frac{52}{5}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{13}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "62dbfd8d-d6c8-5022-be2b-1ea1fd60fadf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508118}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508141 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508093 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b2368f6-3f68-54b0-9d32-a27cbf3895be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+e-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507648} {:answer/id #uuid "b02c68e4-e61b-5654-9879-70de70a8fe6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-2e+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507843} {:answer/id #uuid "b269375e-588f-5e5f-9ae9-591c8a3221c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{2}-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507845} {:answer/id #uuid "bfd68903-0ef9-5e2a-88ed-3677ac3ae07b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507864} {:answer/id #uuid "c01cb739-f7ab-5ebb-8e34-daf477537efd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{2}-5e+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507865}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507864} :answer-field/id #uuid "440eb13c-81c4-5c5d-9faa-611d1e590780" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507967}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b8f3b4b6-eb37-510a-a5f6-48850ac9d684" :question/math-academy-id "q-131140" :question/problem "The continuous random variable $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,1<x<e\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=e-1,$ compute $\\text{E}[2{X}^{2}-X].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that $\\text{E}[aX+bY]=a\\text{E}[X]+b\\text{E}[Y]$ for any random variables $X$ and $Y$ and constants $a$ and $b.$*" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify using the hint and the properties of expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[2{X}^{2}-X]=2\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe already know $\\text{E}[X].$ So, we compute the second raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\text{E}[X]=e-1$ and $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]=\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{2}$ into the simplified expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[2{X}^{2}-X] & =2\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{2}-(e-1) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}-1-e+1 \\\\  & ={e}^{2}-e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "81573813-f419-5bab-b167-8255dfe1a85b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508152}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508148 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508043 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13ed44a6-241d-5838-9fc9-af9b6f83e61d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507622} {:answer/id #uuid "1578c936-8ad1-508e-aa56-b4990c519451" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507624} {:answer/id #uuid "67abf3a2-af43-5891-a4b8-887a1c46a81b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507751} {:answer/id #uuid "7e517eaa-0474-52ae-938e-84dbfaa53de5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507784} {:answer/id #uuid "8dda24db-7965-57a0-84c8-bf571ddd300c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507800}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507624} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5d3c470-c959-5590-9ddc-7ea57ee65776" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0cddfc6e-37ce-5b87-9aa0-8b3fda1d2938" :question/math-academy-id "q-90392" :question/problem "The continuous random variable $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}},\\,x>1\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{3}{2},$ compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}+2X-1].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that $\\text{E}[aX+bY]=a\\text{E}[X]+b\\text{E}[Y]$ for any random variables $X$ and $Y$ and constants $a$ and $b.$*" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify using the hint and the properties of expected value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}+2X-1] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]+2\\text{E}[X]-\\text{E}[1] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]+2\\text{E}[X]-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe already know $\\text{E}[X].$ So, we compute the second raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(-\\frac{3}{x})|}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(3-\\frac{3}{a}) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]=3$ into the simplified expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}+2X-1] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]+2\\text{E}[X]-1 \\\\  & =3+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}-1 \\\\  & =3+3-1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a28c654f-55ef-5916-869c-70e534152c19" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508141}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508152 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434427 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b58a9e1-f2e5-5bab-86c3-1dc62a5bf1dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434407} {:answer/id #uuid "83994b5b-dec3-525b-9473-71abebd50a27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434408} {:answer/id #uuid "908f1924-a4cc-5cae-83ec-6a4c7872904f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434410} {:answer/id #uuid "93d5d0a4-79ad-5a4d-854e-315f284a4eac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434411} {:answer/id #uuid "a25f9bac-857f-5e6a-8fee-76b7ccf2820e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434412}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434408} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c9c34c2-a686-5dff-8116-1e613117e2fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434424}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cf5c8a43-29b3-589c-9d23-ef6cc2a81919" :question/math-academy-id "q-131142" :question/problem "The continuous random variable $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{2},\\,1<x<3\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=2,$ compute $\\text{E}[5X-3{X}^{2}].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that $\\text{E}[aX+bY]=a\\text{E}[X]+b\\text{E}[Y]$ for any random variables $X$ and $Y$ and constants $a$ and $b.$*" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify using the hint and the properties of expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[5X-3{X}^{2}]=5\\text{E}[X]-3\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe already know $\\text{E}[X].$ So, we compute the second raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}\\,|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\text{E}[X]=2$ and $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]=\\frac{13}{3}$ into the simplified expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[5X-3{X}^{2}] & =5\\text{E}[X]-3\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] \\\\  & =5\\cdot 2-3\\cdot \\frac{13}{3} \\\\  & =10-13 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b234828c-5807-5de4-a2a5-5f231ccf4ede" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508137}}] :activity/title "Moments of Continuous Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507600}]}
